-- Q2456: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Contracts for the sale of real property or land interests","Contracts that can be performed within a single calendar year","Contracts for the sale of goods at $500 or more under the UCC","Oral contracts made between immediate family members"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under UCC Section 2-201, contracts for the sale of goods priced at $500 or more must be evidenced by a writing signed by the party to be charged. The Statute of Frauds covers specific categories of contracts, including land sales, suretyship, and contracts not performable within one year.
Wrong (A): Real property contracts do fall under the Statute of Frauds, but the stem asks about the UCC provision specifically, and the correct answer here is goods priced at $500 or more.
Wrong (B): Only contracts that cannot be performed within one year require a writing. Contracts that can be performed within one year are enforceable even if oral.
Wrong (D): There is no Statute of Frauds exception or requirement based on family relationships. The statute applies based on subject matter, not the parties'' relationship.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2456;

-- Q2466: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The contract was a bad bargain for one of the parties","One party''s financial circumstances changed after signing","The contract was induced by duress, fraud, or misrepresentation","One party failed to read the contract before signing it"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts, Section 175, duress involving threats of physical harm or wrongful economic pressure vitiates genuine assent and renders a contract voidable. Similarly, fraud or misrepresentation under Section 164 provides a valid defense where material facts were falsified.
Wrong (A): A bad bargain alone does not invalidate a contract. Courts generally do not inquire into the adequacy of consideration.
Wrong (B): Changed financial circumstances after signing do not constitute a defense to enforcement under contract law.
Wrong (D): Failure to read a contract before signing is not a recognized defense. Parties are presumed to have read and understood terms they agreed to.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2466;

-- Q2470: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$5,000, representing the full contract price for all units","$500, representing 10% of the total contract value","Nothing, because Martin successfully obtained substitute goods","$1,000, the difference between the cover and contract price"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-712, when a seller breaches, the buyer may cover by making a reasonable substitute purchase and recover the difference between the cover price and the contract price. Here, cover damages equal ($12 - $10) x 500 = $1,000.
Wrong (A): The full contract price of $5,000 is not the correct measure. Cover damages compensate only for the price differential, not the entire contract value.
Wrong (B): There is no 10% formula for UCC breach damages. The measure is the actual difference between cover and contract price.
Wrong (C): The ability to cover does not eliminate the right to damages. Cover establishes the measure of recovery, not a bar to it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2470;

-- Q2471: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, the acceptance was effective when dispatched under the mailbox rule","No, because Parker revoked the offer before Quinn could accept it","No, because the revocation was mailed before the acceptance was sent","Yes, but only if Quinn knew of the revocation before accepting"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts, Section 63, an acceptance is effective when properly dispatched (the mailbox rule). A revocation under Section 42 is effective only upon receipt. Quinn dispatched her acceptance Thursday before receiving Parker''s revocation, forming a valid contract.
Wrong (B): The revocation was not effective before Quinn accepted because revocations require actual receipt by the offeree, which had not yet occurred.
Wrong (C): The date of mailing a revocation is irrelevant. Only receipt of the revocation matters, and Quinn had not received it when she dispatched her acceptance.
Wrong (D): The mailbox rule does not require the offeree to be unaware of revocation attempts. It requires only that revocation was not actually received before acceptance was dispatched.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2471;

-- Q2475: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The performance deadline has not expired or the seller reasonably believed the tender was acceptable","The buyer has already used or consumed the delivered goods","More than 30 days have passed since the original delivery date","The buyer expressly accepts the nonconforming goods as delivered"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-508, a seller may cure a nonconforming tender in two situations: (1) if the time for performance has not yet expired, or (2) if the seller had reasonable grounds to believe the tender would be acceptable, in which case additional reasonable time to cure is allowed.
Wrong (B): The buyer''s use of goods does not create a right to cure. Cure depends on timing and the seller''s reasonable belief, not the buyer''s conduct with the goods.
Wrong (C): There is no 30-day statutory period for cure under the UCC. The right depends on whether the performance deadline has passed and the seller''s reasonable expectations.
Wrong (D): If the buyer expressly accepts nonconforming goods, the issue becomes revocation of acceptance under Section 2-608, not the seller''s right to cure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2475;

-- Q2477: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Anticipatory repudiation, allowing the homeowner to treat the contract as breached","A minor breach that entitles the homeowner only to monetary damages","A condition subsequent that automatically terminates the existing contract","Impossibility of performance that legally excuses the painter''s obligation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 253, anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party unequivocally communicates before the performance date that they will not perform. The non-breaching party may immediately treat the contract as breached and pursue remedies.
Wrong (B): A minor breach involves deficient but substantially completed performance. Here, the painter has not performed at all and has declared an intent not to perform, which constitutes total repudiation.
Wrong (C): A condition subsequent is a contractual provision that discharges a duty upon occurrence of a specified event. The painter''s refusal to perform is not a condition subsequent.
Wrong (D): Impossibility requires an unforeseen event making performance objectively impossible. The painter''s voluntary refusal does not qualify as impossibility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2477;

-- Q2484: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is authorized to conduct a broad range of transactions on a continuing basis","Is appointed only for a single, specific transaction or limited task","Has no authority to bind the principal in any contractual dealings","Must always be a salaried employee rather than an independent agent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 2.01, a general agent is authorized to conduct a series of transactions involving a continuity of service for the principal, such as managing an entire business operation on an ongoing basis.
Wrong (B): This describes a special agent, not a general agent. A special agent is appointed for a specific transaction or limited set of tasks.
Wrong (C): Both general and special agents can bind the principal in contracts within their scope of authority. The distinction is breadth of authority, not the ability to bind.
Wrong (D): A general agent need not be an employee. Independent contractors can serve as general agents if granted broad, continuing authority.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2484;

-- Q2497: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Keep accurate records of all funds received and not commingle them","Prepare audited financial statements for the principal annually","File annual federal and state tax returns for the principal","Provide monthly profit and loss statements to the principal"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 8.12, an agent''s duty of accounting requires maintaining accurate records of all property and funds received or expended on the principal''s behalf. The agent must also keep the principal''s assets separate from personal funds.
Wrong (B): Preparing audited financial statements is the role of an independent auditor, not a fiduciary duty imposed on agents by agency law.
Wrong (C): Filing tax returns is not part of the agent''s duty of accounting. Tax compliance is the principal''s responsibility unless specifically delegated.
Wrong (D): Providing profit and loss statements is a management reporting function, not a requirement of the agency law duty of accounting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2497;

-- Q2504: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Pay the agent a bonus for performance that exceeds expectations","Hold the agent harmless from all liability regardless of the agent''s conduct","Provide health insurance and retirement benefits to the agent","Compensate the agent for expenses reasonably incurred within authorized scope"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 8.14, a principal must indemnify the agent for losses and expenses reasonably incurred while acting within the scope of authority. Rivera''s $4,200 in documented travel expenses qualify for reimbursement.
Wrong (A): The duty of indemnification covers authorized expenses, not discretionary bonuses. Bonus compensation is a separate contractual matter.
Wrong (B): Indemnification does not extend to liability caused by the agent''s own negligence or unauthorized conduct. It applies only to losses from authorized activities.
Wrong (C): Providing health insurance and retirement benefits is an employment law matter, not a requirement of the agency law duty of indemnification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2504;

-- Q2515: Business Structures [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A partnership where no partner has personal liability for any obligation","A partnership that is limited to no more than 100 total partners","A partnership that must elect to be taxed as a C corporation","A partnership shielding partners from other partners'' malpractice liability"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RUPA Section 306(c) and state LLP statutes, an LLP provides partners with a liability shield against vicarious liability for the negligence, malpractice, or misconduct of other partners. Each partner remains fully liable for their own acts.
Wrong (A): An LLP does not eliminate all personal liability. Partners remain liable for their own negligence and for obligations they personally guarantee.
Wrong (B): There is no statutory cap of 100 partners for an LLP. The 100-shareholder limit applies to S corporations, not partnerships.
Wrong (C): An LLP retains pass-through partnership taxation and is not required to be taxed as a corporation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2515;

-- Q2520: Business Structures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The corporation has fewer than 10 shareholders on record","The corporation operated only a single line of business","The corporation elected S corporation status for tax purposes","The corporation ignored formalities and served as the owner''s alter ego"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RMBCA Section 6.22 and the alter ego doctrine, courts pierce the corporate veil when the shareholder failed to observe corporate formalities and used the corporation as a personal instrumentality. Commingling personal and corporate funds is a primary factor in this analysis.
Wrong (A): Having few shareholders is common among closely held corporations and is not, by itself, grounds for piercing the corporate veil.
Wrong (B): Operating a single line of business is a legitimate corporate structure and does not indicate the corporation is a sham or alter ego.
Wrong (C): S corporation status is a tax election under IRC Section 1362 and has no bearing on whether the veil should be pierced.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2520;

-- Q2538: Property Transactions: Basis [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A $10,000 recognized gain on the sale of the property","A $15,000 recognized gain on the sale of the property","A $10,000 recognized loss on the sale of the property","No gain or loss is recognized on this transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1015(a) and Treas. Reg. Section 1.1015-1(a)(2), when gift property has FMV below the donor''s basis, a dual-basis rule applies. The gain basis is $80,000 (donor''s basis) and the loss basis is $55,000 (FMV at gift date). Since the $70,000 selling price is below the gain basis but above the loss basis, no gain or loss is recognized.
Wrong (A): A $10,000 gain would require using the loss basis of $55,000, but the gain basis of $80,000 must be used to compute gains. The selling price of $70,000 is below the gain basis.
Wrong (B): A $15,000 gain incorrectly uses the loss basis ($70,000 - $55,000). The gain basis, not the loss basis, applies when determining gain.
Wrong (C): A $10,000 loss would require using the gain basis ($80,000 - $70,000), but the loss basis of $55,000 must be used for losses. The selling price exceeds the loss basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2538;

-- Q2555: Property Transactions: Basis [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Stocks held in the decedent''s brokerage account","Real estate that was owned directly by the decedent","Income in respect of a decedent items such as IRA distributions","A personal automobile owned at the date of death"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1014(c), income in respect of a decedent (IRD) items do not receive a stepped-up basis at death. IRD items such as unpaid wages, IRA distributions, and installment sale receivables retain their tax character and are taxed to the recipient when received.
Wrong (A): Publicly traded stocks held in a brokerage account are eligible for a stepped-up basis to FMV at the date of death under Section 1014(a).
Wrong (B): Real estate owned by the decedent receives a stepped-up basis to FMV at death under Section 1014(a), regardless of any built-in gain.
Wrong (D): A personal automobile, like all tangible property owned at death, receives a stepped-up basis under Section 1014(a).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2555;

-- Q2567: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$7,000 net short-term capital loss, fully deductible this year","$3,000 deductible this year with $4,000 carried forward as short-term","$3,000 ordinary income deduction with no carryforward allowed","$7,000 reclassified as a long-term capital loss for the year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 1211(b), individual taxpayers may deduct up to $3,000 of net capital losses against ordinary income per year. After netting $8,000 in gains against $15,000 in losses, the $7,000 net short-term loss yields a $3,000 deduction with the remaining $4,000 carried forward under Sec. 1212(b), retaining its short-term character.
Wrong (A): Capital losses are not fully deductible. The $3,000 annual limitation applies regardless of the total net loss amount.
Wrong (C): The $3,000 deduction is correct, but unused capital losses do carry forward indefinitely under Sec. 1212(b). There is no forfeiture of excess losses.
Wrong (D): Short-term losses do not convert to long-term losses. Capital losses retain their original holding period character when carried forward.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2567;

-- Q2580: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Short-term and long-term are combined into one category before netting","Only long-term transactions are netted; short-term are treated as ordinary","Losses are deducted first from income, then gains are added separately","Each holding period is netted separately, then the two nets are combined"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1222, capital gains and losses follow a specific netting process: first, net short-term gains against short-term losses; second, net long-term gains against long-term losses; then combine the two net amounts. This ordering matters because short-term gains are taxed at ordinary rates while net long-term gains receive preferential rates.
Wrong (A): Short-term and long-term are not combined into one category. They must be netted separately by holding period before being combined.
Wrong (B): Short-term capital gains and losses are netted under their own category, not treated as ordinary income. Only net short-term gains are taxed at ordinary rates.
Wrong (C): There is no rule requiring losses to be deducted before gains. The process nets gains and losses within each holding period category simultaneously.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2580;

-- Q2627: Individual Taxation: Income [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Jury duty pay received during the tax year","Unemployment compensation benefits received in 2026","Life insurance death proceeds received as a named beneficiary","Cancellation of indebtedness income from a settled debt"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 101(a), life insurance death benefit proceeds received by a beneficiary due to the death of the insured are excluded from gross income. This exclusion applies regardless of the amount received.
Wrong (A): Jury duty pay is included in gross income under Section 61. It is compensation for services and fully taxable.
Wrong (B): Unemployment compensation is included in gross income under IRC Section 85. It has been fully taxable since 2021 for most taxpayers.
Wrong (D): Cancellation of indebtedness income is generally included in gross income under Section 61(a)(11), with limited exceptions for bankruptcy and insolvency under Section 108.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2627;

-- Q2628: Individual Taxation: Income [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$20,000 of COD income included in Victor''s gross income","$50,000 of income equal to the original outstanding balance","$30,000 of income equal to the settlement amount paid","No income because debt settlements are excluded from taxation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 61(a)(11), cancellation of indebtedness income is included in gross income. Victor''s $20,000 of forgiven debt ($50,000 minus $30,000) is taxable because he is neither bankrupt nor insolvent, so the Section 108 exclusions do not apply.
Wrong (B): The taxable amount is only the forgiven portion ($20,000), not the full original debt of $50,000. The amount actually paid is not income.
Wrong (C): The $30,000 settlement payment is not income. Income arises from the $20,000 that was forgiven, not the amount paid to satisfy the debt.
Wrong (D): Debt settlements are generally taxable unless a specific exclusion under Section 108 applies, such as bankruptcy, insolvency, or qualified principal residence debt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2628;

-- Q2639: Individual Taxation: Income [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$12,000, which is exactly 50% of total Social Security benefits","$17,100, computed under the first-tier formula only","$20,400, which is exactly 85% of total Social Security benefits","The lesser of the two-tier formula result or 85% of benefits"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 86(a), the taxable amount of Social Security benefits is determined using a two-tier formula based on provisional income. The calculation yields: 50% of excess over $25,000 up to $34,000 ($4,500) plus 85% of excess over $34,000 ($1,700), totaling $6,200. This is compared to 85% of benefits ($20,400), and the lesser ($6,200) is taxable.
Wrong (A): The 50% figure only applies under the first tier and does not account for the second tier. The actual taxable amount requires applying the full two-tier formula.
Wrong (B): $17,100 is not the result of any correct application of the Section 86 formula for these income levels.
Wrong (C): 85% of benefits ($20,400) is the maximum possible taxable amount, but the actual amount is the lesser of this or the two-tier formula result.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2639;

-- Q2640: Individual Taxation: Income [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All amounts are taxable, but tips under $20/month need not be reported to the employer","All tips are excluded from gross income regardless of amount","Wages are taxable only to the extent they exceed the standard deduction","Salaries and wages are taxable but year-end bonuses are not"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 61, all wages, salaries, bonuses, and tips are included in gross income. However, employees are not required to report tips to their employer if total tips from that employer are less than $20 in a calendar month, though the income remains taxable.
Wrong (B): Tips are not excluded from gross income. All cash and non-cash tips are taxable income under Section 61, regardless of the amount.
Wrong (C): Wages are fully included in gross income. The standard deduction reduces taxable income but does not exclude wages from gross income.
Wrong (D): Bonuses are included in gross income under Section 61, just like wages and salaries. There is no exclusion for bonus compensation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2640;

-- Q2652: Individual Taxation: Deductions [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The itemized deduction is always more beneficial because it allows the full $350 amount","The above-the-line deduction is more valuable because it reduces AGI and is available to all filers","Both deductions affect taxable income identically with no strategic difference between them","The above-the-line deduction phases out at lower income levels than itemized deductions do"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 62(a)(2)(D), the educator expense deduction is an above-the-line adjustment limited to $300. This classification reduces AGI, which cascades through AGI-sensitive provisions such as education credits, the rental loss allowance, and Social Security benefit taxability. It is also available to taxpayers who claim the standard deduction.
Wrong (A): Although the itemized amount ($350) exceeds the above-the-line cap ($300), most educators take the standard deduction, making an itemized-only deduction worthless in practice.
Wrong (C): The two deductions are not identical. Above-the-line deductions reduce AGI, affecting numerous phase-outs, while below-the-line deductions only reduce taxable income.
Wrong (D): The educator expense deduction under Section 62(a)(2)(D) does not phase out based on income. It is a flat dollar limit available to all eligible educators.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2652;

-- Q2694: Individual Taxation: Credits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Tuition payments made directly to the eligible institution","Mandatory student activity fees charged by the university","Course materials such as textbooks and required supplies","Laboratory fees assessed as part of course enrollment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 25A(f)(1)(A), the AOTC''s definition of qualified expenses includes tuition, fees, and course materials (textbooks, supplies, and equipment needed for enrollment). The Lifetime Learning Credit under Section 25A(f)(1)(B) covers only tuition and fees paid to the institution, excluding separately purchased course materials.
Wrong (A): Tuition qualifies for both the AOTC and the Lifetime Learning Credit, so it does not satisfy the question''s condition of qualifying for AOTC but not LLC.
Wrong (B): Student activity fees, when required for enrollment, qualify for both credits. They are not unique to the AOTC.
Wrong (D): Lab fees charged by the institution are treated as enrollment fees and qualify for both the AOTC and the Lifetime Learning Credit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2694;

-- Q2700: Individual Taxation: Credits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The child and dependent care credit only, not the EIC","The earned income credit only, not the care credit","Neither the child and dependent care credit nor the EIC","Both credits may be claimed without any filing limitation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 21(e)(4), married taxpayers filing separately are generally ineligible for the child and dependent care credit. Under IRC Section 32(d), the earned income credit is also disallowed for MFS filers. Both credits require filing as single, HOH, QSS, or MFJ.
Wrong (A): The child and dependent care credit alone is not available. MFS filers are denied this credit under Section 21(e)(4), not just the EIC.
Wrong (B): The earned income credit alone is not available. MFS filers are denied the EIC under Section 32(d), and the care credit is also disallowed.
Wrong (D): Both credits are completely denied under MFS filing status. Unlike the child tax credit, which remains available to MFS filers, these two credits require joint filing or unmarried status.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2700;

-- Q2703: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The phaseout has no effect because Porter''s AMTI is below the completion point","The exemption is reduced by $10,163, and the effective marginal rate rises to about 35%","The exemption is completely eliminated once AMTI exceeds $609,350 by any amount","The phaseout increases the exemption to reward taxpayers subject to the AMT"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 55(d)(3), the AMT exemption is phased out by 25 cents per dollar of AMTI exceeding $609,350. Porter''s $40,650 excess triggers a $10,163 reduction, leaving a $77,937 exemption. Each additional dollar also exposes $0.25 of previously exempt income to the 28% rate, creating an effective marginal rate of approximately 35%.
Wrong (A): Porter''s AMTI of $650,000 exceeds the $609,350 threshold, so the phaseout does apply and reduces the exemption amount.
Wrong (C): The exemption is not eliminated entirely by exceeding the threshold by any amount. The phaseout is gradual at 25 cents per dollar, not a cliff.
Wrong (D): The phaseout reduces the exemption, it does not increase it. Higher AMTI results in a smaller exemption and greater AMT exposure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2703;

-- Q2707: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["U.S. Treasury bonds held in the investment portfolio","General obligation municipal bonds issued by the state","Private activity bonds issued after August 7, 1986","Corporate bonds held in a taxable brokerage account"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 57(a)(5), tax-exempt interest on private activity bonds issued after August 7, 1986 is a tax preference item added back when computing AMTI. These bonds finance projects primarily benefiting private parties rather than the general public.
Wrong (A): U.S. Treasury bond interest is fully taxable for regular tax purposes and is not a tax preference item for AMT. It is already included in taxable income.
Wrong (B): Interest on general obligation municipal bonds remains exempt from both regular tax and AMT. Only private activity bond interest triggers the AMT preference.
Wrong (D): Corporate bond interest is fully taxable under the regular tax and is already included in taxable income, so it is not an AMT preference item.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2707;

-- Q2709: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It is lost permanently and cannot be recovered in any year","It generates an AMT credit to offset regular tax in future years","It is refunded automatically by the IRS without a filing requirement","It can only be used to offset future AMT liability, not regular tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 53, AMT paid due to timing or deferral items such as ISO exercises generates a minimum tax credit (MTC) that carries forward indefinitely. This credit offsets regular tax liability in future years when the regular tax exceeds the tentative minimum tax.
Wrong (A): AMT from timing differences is not permanently lost. The minimum tax credit mechanism ensures taxpayers are not double-taxed on deferral items.
Wrong (C): The AMT credit is not automatically refunded. The taxpayer must claim it on Form 8801 in a year when regular tax exceeds tentative minimum tax.
Wrong (D): The MTC offsets regular tax liability, not future AMT. It is usable to the extent regular tax exceeds tentative minimum tax in a given year.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2709;

-- Q2715: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$88,100, because AMTI is below the phaseout threshold","$0, because the exemption is fully phased out at this level","$60,775, reflecting a partial phaseout of the exemption","$88,100 with no phaseout reduction applied to the amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 55(d), the AMT exemption for single filers is $88,100 in 2026, and it begins to phase out when AMTI exceeds approximately $609,350. Since Karen''s AMTI of $500,000 is below the phaseout threshold, her full $88,100 exemption is available with no reduction.
Wrong (A): While the amount is the same, this choice is a distractor because it does not clearly state that no phaseout applies. The correct answer specifies both the full amount and the absence of phaseout.
Wrong (B): The exemption is not $0. Karen''s AMTI is below the phaseout threshold, so no reduction occurs.
Wrong (C): A $60,775 exemption would imply a partial phaseout, but Karen''s AMTI has not reached the $609,350 threshold where phaseout begins.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2715;

-- Q2716: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It expires permanently when the underlying stock is sold","It converts to a capital loss carryforward on the sale date","It is added to the taxpayer''s adjusted basis in the stock","It offsets regular tax to the extent it exceeds tentative minimum tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 53, the AMT credit from ISO timing preferences carries forward indefinitely and offsets regular tax in any year the regular tax exceeds tentative minimum tax. In the year of sale, the timing difference reverses, typically causing regular tax to exceed AMT and allowing use of the credit.
Wrong (A): The AMT credit does not expire upon sale of the stock. It carries forward indefinitely until fully utilized against regular tax liability.
Wrong (B): The AMT credit does not convert to a capital loss. It remains a credit against regular tax liability, separate from any capital gain or loss on the stock sale.
Wrong (C): The AMT credit is not added to stock basis. The AMT basis adjustment for ISO stock is a separate concept from the minimum tax credit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2716;

-- Q2739: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sandra, because she is the child''s biological mother","Neither parent unless they execute a written agreement","Whichever parent reports the higher adjusted gross income","The ex-husband, because the child lived with him over half the year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 152(c)(4)(A), when a child qualifies under both parents'' households, the child is treated as the qualifying child of the parent with whom the child resided for the greater portion of the year. Since the son lived with the ex-husband 60% of the year, he is the custodial parent.
Wrong (A): The dependency claim is not automatically awarded to the mother. The tiebreaker rule is based on time of residence, not the parent''s gender.
Wrong (B): A written agreement is not required for the custodial parent to claim the child. The residence tiebreaker applies by default, though Form 8332 allows a voluntary release.
Wrong (C): Income is a secondary tiebreaker under Section 152(c)(4)(C) used only when both parents have equal custody time. Here, the residence test resolves the claim.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2739;

-- Q2743: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because Nancy has no earned income for the year","Yes, if they lived apart the last 6 months with a dependent child","No, because married taxpayers are ineligible for head of household","No, because head of household status requires being legally unmarried"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 7703(b), a married taxpayer may be considered unmarried and file as head of household if they lived apart from their spouse for the last six months of the year and maintained a home for a qualifying child for more than half the year. Both conditions must be met.
Wrong (A): Nancy''s lack of income is irrelevant to head of household eligibility. The requirements focus on living apart and maintaining a household for a qualifying child.
Wrong (C): Married taxpayers can file as head of household if they meet the considered-unmarried rules under Section 7703(b). This filing status is not limited to those who are legally single.
Wrong (D): Head of household does not strictly require being unmarried. The considered-unmarried exception allows certain separated married taxpayers to qualify.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2743;

-- Q2753: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Child tax credit if the qualifying child lives with the taxpayer","Earned income credit based on wages and earned income","Full student loan interest deduction up to the annual limit","Child and dependent care credit for qualified care expenses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 24(a), a married filing separately taxpayer may claim the child tax credit for qualifying children who live with them. The phaseout for MFS filers begins at $200,000 AGI, making it one of the few family credits available under this filing status.
Wrong (B): The earned income credit is completely disallowed for MFS filers under IRC Section 32(d), regardless of income level or qualifying children.
Wrong (C): The student loan interest deduction under IRC Section 221(e)(1) is not available to taxpayers filing as married filing separately.
Wrong (D): The child and dependent care credit is denied to MFS filers under IRC Section 21(e)(4), with a narrow exception if the taxpayer qualifies as considered unmarried.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2753;
